{=============================================================================
  Flow and film boiling inside tubes: heat-transfer coefficients
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0098 (function library, kind = function)
  -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Ten correlations for the two-phase (flow-boiling) heat-transfer coefficient h_tp
  of a fluid flowing and boiling inside a tube, all returning h_tp [W/m^2/K]:

    from ht (heat transfer component of ChEDL), flow and film boiling:
    - h_Lazarek_Black          film boiling, vertical tube, upward or downward
    - h_Li_Wu                 film boiling, any orientation of flow
    - h_Sun_Mishima           film boiling, any orientation of flow
    - h_Thome                 flow boiling with hydrodynamic slug model
    - h_Yun_Heo_Kim           flow boiling, any orientation of flow
    - h_Chen_Edelstein        convective + nucleate, Chen 1966 / Edelstein 1984
    - h_Chen_Bennett          convective + nucleate, Bennett & Chen 1980
    - h_Liu_Winterton         convective + nucleate, Liu & Winterton 1991

    from the ThermoCycle Modelica library (Shah 1982 and Gungor-Winterton 1987,
    which ht does not have):
    - h_Shah_1982             saturated boiling chart correlation, Shah 1982
    - h_Gungor_Winterton_1987 simplified turbulent flow boiling, 1987

  Every function takes the mass flow rate, the quality (where the correlation
  needs it), the tube diameter and the liquid and vapour properties as arguments:
  no property function is called inside a function, so the calling model passes
  the properties itself (as everywhere else in this library).

  Four correlations (Lazarek-Black, Li-Wu, Sun-Mishima, Yun-Heo-Kim) are given
  either as a function of the wall heat flux q or of the excess wall temperature
  Te = T_wall - T_sat; the last form is the algebraic inversion of the first and
  is selected with the flag by_Te (1 = Te given, 0 = q given), as in ht, where
  the two inputs are optional. h_Thome takes q only: in ht the Te form solves an
  inner equation for q (a secant iteration); here q is an additional unknown of
  the calling system, with the equation h_Thome(...) = Te (see the demonstration
  program and the README).

  Three correlations call other correlations of the library, which are written
  out in the body (CoolSolve cannot import the functions of another library file
  yet, CS-FEAT-IMPORT): the Dittus-Boelter coefficient (CSL-0087, functions
  Nu_Dittus_Boelter and Nu_Gnielinski), the Forster-Zuber and Cooper pool-boiling
  coefficients (CSL-0088, functions h_Forster_Zuber and h_Cooper).

  The demonstration program at the end calls every correlation: case A with the
  input set of the ht doctest of the corresponding correlation, case B with one
  uniform, realistic input set (R134a at 5 C, 0.2 kg/s, quality 0.4, 4E4 W/m^2,
  10 mm tube), case C with a low liquid Froude number (only for the two
  ThermoCycle correlations, where it separates the vertical and the horizontal
  form of Shah 1982). Both cases are tabulated against ht in the README.

  Inputs : m [kg/s], x [-], D [m], q [W/m^2], Te [K], dPsat [Pa], i_fg [J/kg],
           rhol / rhog [kg/m^3], mul / mug [Pa-s], kl / kg [W/m-K],
           Cpl / Cpg [J/kg-K], Hvap [J/kg], sigma [N/m], Psat / P / Pc [Pa],
           MW [g/mol], vertical / by_Te flags [-]
  Outputs: h_tp [W/m^2/K] (one FUNCTION per correlation)
  Units  : SI - temperatures in K, pressures in Pa
  Source : ht (Caleb Bell), ht/boiling_flow.py, version 1.2.0, commit 85e0ee6,
           MIT - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht ; ThermoCycle (Modelica, MIT),
           Components/HeatFlow/HeatTransfer/TwoPhaseCorrelations/Shah1982.mo and
           GungorWinterton1987.mo ; correlations by the authors cited in each
           function block - see README.md
  Status : VERIFIED against the doctests and the values of ht 1.2.0 for the eight
           ht correlations, and against a Python transcription of the two
           ThermoCycle equations for the other two (README.md, "Verification")
=============================================================================}

FUNCTION tr_factor_Richter2(start, stop, position)
{TR_FACTOR_RICHTER2 - weight of a smooth transition from 0 at start to 1 at
stop, the blending function that the Shah 1982 correlation uses in ThermoCycle.

  tFactor = 0                                             position <= start
          = 1                                             position >= stop
          = 1 - [a*(1/3*cos(phi)^2*sin(phi) + 2/3*sin(phi)) + b]
  phi = (position - x_t)/Delta_x*pi, x_t = start + Delta_x/2, a = -3/4, b = 1/2
        (Delta_x = stop - start; order 2 = two continuous derivatives)

start, stop [dimensionless] bounds of the transition interval, position [-] the
variable being switched. SIN and COS take their argument in degrees, everywhere
in EES and in CoolSolve, so phi is written in degrees.
Original: Richter, C. C., "Proposal of New Object-Oriented Equation-Based Model
  Libraries for Thermodynamic Systems", PhD thesis, Technische Universitaet
  Braunschweig, 2008, p. 68 ff (the transition function of order 2).
Taken from: ThermoCycle (Modelica, MIT), Functions/transition_factor.mo, function
  transition_factor, default order = 2, as called by
  TwoPhaseCorrelations/Shah1982.mo}
  Delta_x = stop - start
  x_t = start + 0.5*Delta_x
  IF position <= start THEN
    tr_factor_Richter2 = 0
  ELSE
    IF position >= stop THEN
      tr_factor_Richter2 = 1
    ELSE
      phi = (position - x_t)/Delta_x*180  "phi in degrees, so that SIN and COS apply"
      tr_factor_Richter2 = 1 - (-0.75*(1/3*COS(phi)^2*SIN(phi) + 2/3*SIN(phi)) + 0.5)
    ENDIF
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION h_Lazarek_Black(m, D, mul, kl, Hvap, q, Te, by_Te)
{H_LAZAREK_BLACK - heat-transfer coefficient of the film boiling of a saturated
fluid in a vertical tube, upward or downward flow. Quality independent, no gas
property required, Reynolds number assuming all the flow is liquid.

  h_tp = 30*Re_lo^0.857*Bg^0.714*kl/D
  Re_lo = G*D/mul,  Bg = q/(G*Hvap),  G = m/(pi/4*D^2)

m [kg/s] mass flow rate, D [m] diameter of the channel, mul [Pa-s] viscosity of
the liquid, kl [W/m-K] thermal conductivity of the liquid, Hvap [J/kg] heat of
vaporization, q [W/m^2] heat flux to the wall, Te [K] excess temperature of the
wall. by_Te = 0 uses q, by_Te = 1 uses the algebraic inversion for a prescribed
excess temperature (the second form of ht, derived with sympy).
Validity: developed for quality 0 to 0.6, Re_lo 860 to 5500, mass flux 125 to
  750 kg/m^2/s, q of 1.4 to 38 W/cm^2, pipe diameter 3.1 mm, data with R113 only
  (as quoted in ht).
Original: Lazarek, G. M. and S. H. Black, "Evaporative Heat Transfer, Pressure
  Drop and Critical Heat Flux in a Small Vertical Tube with R-113", Int. J. Heat
  Mass Transfer 25(7): 945-960, July 1982; also reviewed by Fang, X., Z. Zhou
  and D. Li, "Review of Correlations of Flow Boiling Heat Transfer Coefficients
  for Carbon Dioxide", Int. J. Refrigeration 36(8): 2017-2039, December 2013, and
  by Bertsch, S. S., E. A. Groll and S. V. Garimella, "Review and Comparative
  Analysis of Studies on Saturated Flow Boiling in Small Channels", Nanoscale
  Microscale Thermophys. Eng. 12(3): 187-227, 2008.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_flow.py, Lazarek_Black,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)  "two-phase mass flux [kg/m^2/s]"
  Relo = G*D/mul
  IF by_Te = 0 THEN
    Bg = q/(G*Hvap)  "boiling number [-]"
    h_Lazarek_Black = 30*Relo^0.857*Bg^0.714*kl/D
  ELSE
    h_Lazarek_Black = 27000*30^(71/143)*(1/(G*Hvap))^(357/143)*Relo^(857/286)*Te^(357/143)*kl^(500/143)/D^(500/143)
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION h_Li_Wu(m, x, D, rhol, rhog, mul, kl, Hvap, sigma, q, Te, by_Te)
{H_LI_WU - heat-transfer coefficient of the film boiling of a saturated fluid in
a tube, any orientation of flow. Uses the liquid Reynolds number, the Bond
number and the boiling number.

  h_tp = 334*Bg^0.3*(Bo*Re_l^0.36)^0.4*kl/D
  Re_l = G*(1-x)*D/mul,  Bo = g*(rhol-rhog)*D^2/sigma,  Bg = q/(G*Hvap)

m [kg/s] mass flow rate, x [-] quality at the specific tube interval, D [m]
diameter of the tube, rhol / rhog [kg/m^3] densities of the liquid and of the
gas, mul [Pa-s] viscosity of the liquid, kl [W/m-K] thermal conductivity of the
liquid, Hvap [J/kg] heat of vaporization, sigma [N/m] surface tension of the
liquid, q [W/m^2] heat flux to the wall, Te [K] excess temperature of the wall.
by_Te = 0 uses q, by_Te = 1 the algebraic inversion (the second form of ht).
Validity: hydraulic diameters from 0.19 to 3.1 mm and 12 different fluids, from
  18 sets of experimental data (as quoted in ht); no numeric range given.
Original: Li, W. and Z. Wu, "A General Correlation for Evaporative Heat Transfer
  in Micro/mini-Channels", Int. J. Heat Mass Transfer 53(9-10): 1778-1787,
  April 2010; also reviewed by Fang, Zhou & Li (2013) and by Kim, S.-M. and
  I. Mudawar, "Review of Databases and Predictive Methods for Pressure Drop in
  Adiabatic, Condensing and Boiling Mini/micro-Channel Flows", Int. J. Heat Mass
  Transfer 77: 74-97, October 2014.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_flow.py, Li_Wu,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  g_val = 9.80665  "gravitational acceleration [m/s^2]"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)  "two-phase mass flux [kg/m^2/s]"
  Rel = G*D*(1-x)/mul
  Bo = g_val*(rhol-rhog)*D^2/sigma  "bond number [-]"
  IF by_Te = 0 THEN
    Bg = q/(G*Hvap)  "boiling number [-]"
    h_Li_Wu = 334*Bg^0.3*(Bo*Rel^0.36)^0.4*kl/D
  ELSE
    A = 334*(Bo*Rel^0.36)^0.4*kl/D
    h_Li_Wu = A^(10/7)*Te^(3/7)/(G^(3/7)*Hvap^(3/7))
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION h_Sun_Mishima(m, D, rhol, rhog, mul, kl, Hvap, sigma, q, Te, by_Te)
{H_SUN_MISHIMA - heat-transfer coefficient of the film boiling of a saturated
fluid in a tube, any orientation of flow. Uses the liquid-only Reynolds number,
the Weber number (with the velocity if all the fluid were liquid) and the boiling
number.

  h_tp = 6*Re_lo^1.05*Bg^0.54/[We_l^0.191*(rhol/rhog)^0.142]*kl/D
  Re_lo = G*D/mul,  We_l = V^2*D*rhol/sigma,  V = G/rhol,  Bg = q/(G*Hvap)

m [kg/s] mass flow rate, D [m] diameter of the tube, rhol / rhog [kg/m^3]
densities of the liquid and of the gas, mul [Pa-s] viscosity of the liquid, kl
[W/m-K] thermal conductivity of the liquid, Hvap [J/kg] heat of vaporization,
sigma [N/m] surface tension of the liquid, q [W/m^2] heat flux to the wall, Te
[K] excess temperature of the wall. by_Te = 0 uses q, by_Te = 1 the algebraic
inversion (the second form of ht).
Validity: hydraulic diameters from 0.21 to 6.05 mm and 11 different fluids, from
  2501 data points (as quoted in ht); no numeric range given.
Original: Sun, L. and K. Mishima, "An Evaluation of Prediction Methods for
  Saturated Flow Boiling Heat Transfer in Mini-Channels", Int. J. Heat Mass
  Transfer 52(23-24): 5323-5329, November 2009; also reviewed by Fang, Zhou &
  Li (2013).
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_flow.py, Sun_Mishima,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)  "two-phase mass flux [kg/m^2/s]"
  V = G/rhol
  Relo = G*D/mul
  We = V^2*D*rhol/sigma  "weber number [-]"
  IF by_Te = 0 THEN
    Bg = q/(G*Hvap)  "boiling number [-]"
    h_Sun_Mishima = 6*Relo^1.05*Bg^0.54/(We^0.191*(rhol/rhog)^0.142)*kl/D
  ELSE
    A = 6*Relo^1.05/(We^0.191*(rhol/rhog)^0.142)*kl/D
    h_Sun_Mishima = A^(50/23)*Te^(27/23)/(G^(27/23)*Hvap^(27/23))
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION h_Thome(m, x, D, rhol, rhog, mul, mug, kl, kg, Cpl, Cpg, Hvap, sigma, Psat, Pc, q)
{H_THOME - heat-transfer coefficient of the flow boiling of a saturated fluid in
a tube, any orientation of flow; the Thome model adds a hydrodynamic layer with
liquid, film and dry (vapour) segments to the laminar, transitional and turbulent
convection of each phase. The heat flux q is an input: for a prescribed excess
wall temperature Te, ht solves an inner equation for q (to_solve_q_Thome, a secant
iteration); here q is an additional unknown of the calling system with the
equation h_Thome(...) = Te (see the demonstration program).

  h(z) = tl/tau*h_l(z) + t_film/tau*h_film(z) + t_dry/tau*h_g(z)
  h_l/g(z) = (Nu_lam^4 + Nu_trans^4)^(1/4)*k/D
  Nu_lam = 0.91*Pr^(1/3)*sqrt(Re*D/L(z))
  Nu_trans = (f/8)(Re-1000)*Pr/[1 + 12.7*(f/8)^0.5*(Pr^(2/3)-1)]*[1 + (D/L(z))^(2/3)]
  f = (1.82*log10(Re) - 1.64)^(-2)
  L_l = tau*G*(1-x)/rhol,  L_dry = v_p*t_dry
  t_l = tau/[1 + (rhol/rhog)*x/(1-x)],  t_v = tau/[1 + (rhog/rhol)*(1-x)/x]
  tau = 1/f_opt,  f_opt = (q/q_ref)^n_f,  q_ref = 3328*(Psat/Pc)^(-0.5), n_f = 1.74
  h_film(z) = 2*kl/[delta_0(z) + delta_min],  delta_min = 0.3E-6 m
  delta_0/D = C_delta0*(3*sqrt(nul/(v_p*D)))^0.84*[(0.07*Bo^0.41)^(-8) + 0.1^(-8)]^(-1/8)
  Bo = rhol*D/sigma*v_p^2  (the Bond number of the model, not the usual one)
  v_p = G*[x/rhog + (1-x)/rhol],  C_delta0 = 0.29

m [kg/s] mass flow rate, x [-] quality at the specific tube interval, D [m]
diameter of the tube, rhol / rhog [kg/m^3] densities of the liquid and of the
gas, mul / mug [Pa-s] viscosities, kl / kg [W/m-K] thermal conductivities, Cpl /
Cpg [J/kg-K] heat capacities, Hvap [J/kg] heat of vaporization, sigma [N/m]
surface tension of the liquid, Psat [Pa] vapor pressure of the fluid, Pc [Pa]
critical pressure, q [W/m^2] heat flux to the wall.
Validity: data from 7 studies, 7 fluids, D_h from 0.7 to 3.1 mm, heat flux from
  0.5 to 17.8 W/cm^2, quality from 0.01 to 0.99, mass flux from 50 to 564 kg/m^2/s
  (as quoted in ht).
Original: Thome, J. R., V. Dupont and A. M. Jacobi, "Heat Transfer Model for
  Evaporation in Microchannels. Part I: Presentation of the Model", Int. J. Heat
  Mass Transfer 47(14-16): 3375-3385, July 2004, and Part II, same volume,
  3387-3401; also reviewed by Bertsch, Groll & Garimella (2008).
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_flow.py, Thome,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  C_delta0 = 0.3E-6  "delta_min of the model [m]"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)  "two-phase mass flux [kg/m^2/s]"
  Rel = G*D*(1-x)/mul
  Reg = G*D*x/mug
  qref = 3328*(Psat/Pc)^(-0.5)  "reference heat flux of the model [W/m^2]"
  fopt = (q/qref)^1.74
  tau = 1/fopt  "residence time of a slug [s]"
  vp = G*(x/rhog + (1-x)/rhol)  "velocity of the slug [m/s]"
  Bo = rhol*D/sigma*vp^2  "bond number of the model [-]"
  nul = mul/rhol  "kinematic viscosity of the liquid [m^2/s]"
  delta0 = D*0.29*(3*(nul/vp/D)^0.5)^0.84*((0.07*Bo^0.41)^(-8) + 0.1^(-8))^(-1/8)  "initial film thickness [m]"
  tl = tau/(1 + rhol/rhog*(x/(1-x)))
  tv = tau/(1 + rhog/rhol*((1-x)/x))
  t_dry_film = rhol*Hvap/q*(delta0 - C_delta0)
  IF t_dry_film > tv THEN
    t_film = tv  "the film lasts the whole vapour slug"
    t_dry = 0
  ELSE
    t_film = t_dry_film
    t_dry = tv - t_film
  ENDIF
  Ll = tau*G/rhol*(1-x)  "length of a liquid slug [m]"
  Ldry = t_dry*vp  "length of a dry (vapour) slug [m]"
  Prg = mug*Cpg/kg
  Prl = mul*Cpl/kl
  fg = (1.82*LOG10(Reg) - 1.64)^(-2)  "friction factor of the vapour slug [-]"
  fl = (1.82*LOG10(Rel) - 1.64)^(-2)  "friction factor of the liquid slug [-]"
  Nu_lam_Zl = 2*0.455*Prl^(1/3)*(D*Rel/Ll)^0.5  "as in ht (twice 0.455)"
  Nu_trans_Zl = (fl/8)*(Rel - 1000)*Prl/(1 + 12.7*(fl/8)^0.5*(Prl^(2/3) - 1))*(1 + (D/Ll)^(2/3))  "Gnielinski, CSL-0087"
  IF Ldry = 0 THEN
    Nu_lam_Zg = 0
    Nu_trans_Zg = 0
  ELSE
    Nu_lam_Zg = 2*0.455*Prg^(1/3)*(D*Reg/Ldry)^0.5
    Nu_trans_Zg = (fg/8)*(Reg - 1000)*Prg/(1 + 12.7*(fg/8)^0.5*(Prg^(2/3) - 1))*(1 + (D/Ldry)^(2/3))
  ENDIF
  h_Zl = kl/D*(Nu_lam_Zl^4 + Nu_trans_Zl^4)^0.25
  h_Zg = kg/D*(Nu_lam_Zg^4 + Nu_trans_Zg^4)^0.25
  h_film = 2*kl/(delta0 + C_delta0)
  h_Thome = tl/tau*h_Zl + t_film/tau*h_film + t_dry/tau*h_Zg
END

FUNCTION h_Yun_Heo_Kim(m, x, D, rhol, mul, Hvap, sigma, q, Te, by_Te)
{H_YUN_HEO_KIM - heat-transfer coefficient of the flow boiling of a saturated
fluid in a tube, any orientation of flow. Uses the liquid Reynolds number, the
Weber number (velocity of the liquid if all the flow were liquid) and the boiling
number.

  h_tp = 136876*(Bg*We_l)^0.1993*Re_l^(-0.1626)
  Re_l = G*D*(1-x)/mul,  We_l = G^2*D/(rhol*sigma),  Bg = q/(G*Hvap)

m [kg/s] mass flow rate, x [-] quality at the specific tube interval, D [m]
diameter of the tube, rhol [kg/m^3] density of the liquid, mul [Pa-s] viscosity
of the liquid, Hvap [J/kg] heat of vaporization, sigma [N/m] surface tension of
the liquid, q [W/m^2] heat flux to the wall, Te [K] excess temperature of the
wall. by_Te = 0 uses q, by_Te = 1 the algebraic inversion (the second form of ht).
Validity: none quoted in ht.
Original: Yun, R., J. H. Heo and Y. Kim, "Evaporative Heat Transfer and Pressure
  Drop of R410A in Microchannels", Int. J. Refrigeration 29(1): 92-100, January
  2006, with the erratum Int. J. Refrigeration 30(8): 1468, December 2007; also
  reviewed by Bertsch, Groll & Garimella (2008).
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_flow.py, Yun_Heo_Kim,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)  "two-phase mass flux [kg/m^2/s]"
  V = G/rhol
  Rel = G*D*(1-x)/mul
  We = V^2*D*rhol/sigma  "weber number [-]"
  IF by_Te = 0 THEN
    Bg = q/(G*Hvap)  "boiling number [-]"
    h_Yun_Heo_Kim = 136876*(Bg*We)^0.1993*Rel^(-0.1626)
  ELSE
    A = 136876*We^0.1993*Rel^(-0.1626)*(Te/G/Hvap)^0.1993
    h_Yun_Heo_Kim = A^(10000/8007)
  ENDIF
END

FUNCTION h_Chen_Edelstein(m, x, D, rhol, rhog, mul, mug, kl, Cpl, Hvap, sigma, dPsat, Te)
{H_CHEN_EDELSTEIN - heat-transfer coefficient of a saturated fluid boiling with
forced convection in a tube, combining the Dittus-Boelter single-phase
coefficient of the liquid and the Forster-Zuber nucleate-pool-boiling
coefficient with two factors F and S.

  h_tp = S*h_nb + F*h_sp,l
  h_sp,l = 0.023*Re_l^0.8*Pr_l^0.4*kl/D,  Re_l = D*G*(1-x)/mul
  h_nb = 0.00122*(kl^0.79*Cpl^0.45*rhol^0.49/[sigma^0.5*mul^0.29*Hvap^0.24*rhog^0.24])*Te^0.24*dPsat^0.75
  F = (1 + X_tt^(-0.5))^1.78
  X_tt = [(1-x)/x]^0.9*(rhog/rhol)^0.5*(mul/mug)^0.1
  S = 0.9622 - 0.5822*atan(Re_l*F^1.25/(6.18E4))

m [kg/s] mass flow rate, x [-] quality at the specific tube interval, D [m]
diameter of the tube, rhol / rhog [kg/m^3] densities of the liquid and of the
gas, mul / mug [Pa-s] viscosities, kl [W/m-K] thermal conductivity of the liquid,
Cpl [J/kg-K] heat capacity of the liquid, Hvap [J/kg] heat of vaporization,
sigma [N/m] surface tension of the liquid, dPsat [Pa] difference between the
saturation pressure at the wall temperature and at the saturation temperature,
Te [K] excess temperature of the wall. ATAN returns degrees in EES and in
CoolSolve, so the angle is converted to radians.
Validity: none quoted in ht; the model is only put together in the review of
  Bertsch, Groll & Garimella (2008); many other forms of this equation exist
  with different functions for F and S.
Original: Chen, J. C., "Correlation for Boiling Heat Transfer to Saturated
  Fluids in Convective Flow", Ind. Eng. Chem. Process Des. Dev. 5(3): 322-329,
  1 July 1966; Edelstein, S., A. J. Perez and J. C. Chen, "Analytic
  Representation of Convective Boiling Functions", AIChE Journal 30(5): 840-841,
  1 September 1984; Bertsch, Groll & Garimella (2008).
The single-phase and pool-boiling coefficients are CSL-0087 (Nu_Dittus_Boelter)
  and CSL-0088 (h_Forster_Zuber); the Lockhart-Martinelli parameter is computed
  here, as in ht.
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_flow.py, Chen_Edelstein,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)  "two-phase mass flux [kg/m^2/s]"
  Rel = D*G*(1-x)/mul
  Prl = mul*Cpl/kl
  hl = 0.023*Rel^0.8*Prl^0.4*kl/D  "Dittus-Boelter of the liquid (CSL-0087), heating and revised"
  Xtt = ((1-x)/x)^0.9*(rhog/rhol)^0.5*(mul/mug)^0.1  "Lockhart-Martinelli parameter [-]"
  F = (1 + Xtt^(-0.5))^1.78
  S = 0.9622 - 0.5822*ATAN(Rel*F^1.25/6.18E4)*pi_val/180  "ATAN gives degrees"
  hnb = 0.00122*(kl^0.79*Cpl^0.45*rhol^0.49/sigma^0.5/mul^0.29/Hvap^0.24/rhog^0.24)*Te^0.24*dPsat^0.75  "Forster-Zuber (CSL-0088)"
  h_Chen_Edelstein = hnb*S + hl*F
END

FUNCTION h_Chen_Bennett(m, x, D, rhol, rhog, mul, mug, kl, Cpl, Hvap, sigma, dPsat, Te)
{H_CHEN_BENNETT - heat-transfer coefficient of a saturated fluid boiling with
forced convection in a tube; the same model as h_Chen_Edelstein with the factors F
and S of Bennett & Chen (1980).

  h_tp = S*h_nb + F*h_sp,l
  h_sp,l = 0.023*Re_l^0.8*Pr_l^0.4*kl/D,  Re_l = D*G*(1-x)/mul
  h_nb = 0.00122*(kl^0.79*Cpl^0.45*rhol^0.49/[sigma^0.5*mul^0.29*Hvap^0.24*rhog^0.24])*Te^0.24*dPsat^0.75
  F = [(Pr_l+1)/2]^0.444*(1 + X_tt^(-0.5))^1.78
  X_tt = [(1-x)/x]^0.9*(rhog/rhol)^0.5*(mul/mug)^0.1
  X_0 = 0.041*[sigma/(g*(rhol-rhog))]^0.5
  S = [1 - exp(-F*h_sp,l*X_0/kl)]/[F*h_sp,l*X_0/kl]

m [kg/s] mass flow rate, x [-] quality at the specific tube interval, D [m]
diameter of the tube, rhol / rhog [kg/m^3] densities of the liquid and of the
gas, mul / mug [Pa-s] viscosities, kl [W/m-K] thermal conductivity of the liquid,
Cpl [J/kg-K] heat capacity of the liquid, Hvap [J/kg] heat of vaporization,
sigma [N/m] surface tension of the liquid, dPsat [Pa] difference between the
saturation pressure at the wall temperature and at the saturation temperature,
Te [K] excess temperature of the wall.
Validity: none quoted in ht; many other forms of this equation exist with
  different functions for F and S.
Original: Bennett, D. L. and J. C. Chen, "Forced Convective Boiling in Vertical
  Tubes for Saturated Pure Components and Binary Mixtures", AIChE Journal 26(3):
  454-461, 1 May 1980, and Bennett, D. L., M. W. Davies and B. L. Hertzler, "The
  Suppression of Saturated Nucleate Boiling by Forced Convective Flow", AIChE
  Symposium Series 76(199): 91-103, 1980; Bertsch, Groll & Garimella (2008).
The single-phase and pool-boiling coefficients are CSL-0087 (Nu_Dittus_Boelter)
  and CSL-0088 (h_Forster_Zuber).
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_flow.py, Chen_Bennett,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  g_val = 9.80665  "gravitational acceleration [m/s^2]"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)  "two-phase mass flux [kg/m^2/s]"
  Rel = D*G*(1-x)/mul
  Prl = mul*Cpl/kl
  hl = 0.023*Rel^0.8*Prl^0.4*kl/D  "Dittus-Boelter of the liquid (CSL-0087), heating and revised"
  Xtt = ((1-x)/x)^0.9*(rhog/rhol)^0.5*(mul/mug)^0.1  "Lockhart-Martinelli parameter [-]"
  F = ((Prl+1)/2)^0.444*(1 + Xtt^(-0.5))^1.78
  X0 = 0.041*(sigma/(g_val*(rhol-rhog)))^0.5  "critical quality of the interfacial instability [m]"
  S = (1 - EXP(-F*hl*X0/kl))/(F*hl*X0/kl)
  hnb = 0.00122*(kl^0.79*Cpl^0.45*rhol^0.49/sigma^0.5/mul^0.29/Hvap^0.24/rhog^0.24)*Te^0.24*dPsat^0.75  "Forster-Zuber (CSL-0088)"
  h_Chen_Bennett = hnb*S + hl*F
END

FUNCTION h_Liu_Winterton(m, x, D, rhol, rhog, mul, kl, Cpl, MW, P, Pc, Te)
{H_LIU_WINTERTON - heat-transfer coefficient of a saturated fluid boiling with
forced convection in a tube: the convective coefficient of the liquid and the
Cooper nucleate-pool-boiling coefficient, combined in quadrature.

  h_tp = sqrt[(F*h_l)^2 + (S*h_nb)^2]
  S = [1 + 0.055*F^0.1*Re_L^0.16]^(-1)
  h_l = 0.023*Re_L^0.8*Pr_l^0.4*kl/D,  Re_L = G*D/mul
  F = [1 + x*Pr_l*(rhol/rhog - 1)]^0.35
  h_nb = {55*Te^0.67*(P/Pc)^[0.12 - 0.2*log10(R_p)]*[-log10(P/Pc)]^(-0.55)*MW^(-0.5)}^(1/0.33)

m [kg/s] mass flow rate, x [-] quality at the specific tube interval, D [m]
diameter of the tube, rhol / rhog [kg/m^3] densities of the liquid and of the
gas, mul [Pa-s] viscosity of the liquid, kl [W/m-K] thermal conductivity of the
liquid, Cpl [J/kg-K] heat capacity of the liquid, MW [g/mol] molar mass, P [Pa]
pressure of the fluid, Pc [Pa] critical pressure, Te [K] excess temperature of
the wall. The roughness Rp of the Cooper pool-boiling term is the 1 micrometre
default of ht (it enters only through its logarithm); the Reynolds number of h_l
assumes all the flow to be liquid, as in ht.
Validity: none quoted in ht. A correction for horizontal flow at low Froude
  numbers exists in the original but is not recommended in several sources and is
  not implemented in ht.
Original: Liu, Z. and R. H. S. Winterton, "A General Correlation for Saturated
  and Subcooled Flow Boiling in Tubes and Annuli, Based on a Nucleate Pool Boiling
  Equation", Int. J. Heat Mass Transfer 34(11): 2759-2766, November 1991; also
  reviewed by Fang, Zhou & Li (2013) and by Bertsch, Groll & Garimella (2008).
The single-phase coefficient is CSL-0087 (Nu_Dittus_Boelter) and the pool-boiling
  coefficient is CSL-0088 (h_Cooper).
Taken from: ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/boiling_flow.py, Liu_Winterton,
  MIT License, C. Bell - https://github.com/CalebBell/ht}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)  "two-phase mass flux [kg/m^2/s]"
  ReL = D*G/mul
  Prl = mul*Cpl/kl
  hl = 0.023*ReL^0.8*Prl^0.4*kl/D  "Dittus-Boelter of the liquid (CSL-0087), heating and revised"
  F = (1 + x*Prl*(rhol/rhog - 1))^0.35
  S = (1 + 0.055*F^0.1*ReL^0.16)^(-1)
  Pr_r = P/Pc  "reduced pressure [-]"
  Rp_um = 1  "surface roughness [micrometres], the default of ht"
  hnb = (55*Te^0.67*Pr_r^(0.12 - 0.2*LOG10(Rp_um))*(-LOG10(Pr_r))^(-0.55)*MW^(-0.5))^(1/0.33)  "Cooper (CSL-0088)"
  h_Liu_Winterton = ((F*hl)^2 + (S*hnb)^2)^0.5
END

FUNCTION h_Shah_1982(m, x, D, q, rhol, rhog, mul, kl, Cpl, i_fg, vertical)
{H_SHAH_1982 - heat-transfer coefficient of a saturated fluid boiling inside a
tube, from the chart correlation of Shah (1982): the enhancement factor psi =
h_tp/h_l of the liquid-only Dittus-Boelter coefficient is built from a
convective term and a nucleate term, each of them taking the larger of its
correlations, blended smoothly where they cross.

  psi = h_tp/h_l (Eq. 1)
  Co = (1/x - 1)^0.8*sqrt(rhog/rhol) (Eq. 2)
  Bo = q/(G*i_fg) (Eq. 3)
  Fr_l = G^2/(rhol^2*g*D) (Eq. 4)
  h_l = 0.023*Re_l^0.8*Pr_l^0.4*kl/D (Eq. 5, Dittus-Boelter of the liquid flow)
  psi_nb = 230*sqrt(Bo) (Eq. 6) or 1 + 46*sqrt(Bo) (Eq. 7)
  psi_cb = 1.8/N^0.8 (Eq. 8)
  psi_nbbs = F*sqrt(Bo)*exp(2.74*N^(-0.1)) (Eq. 9)
  psi_bs = F*sqrt(Bo)*exp(2.47*N^(-0.15)) (Eq. 10)
  F = 14.70 (Eq. 11) or 15.43 (Eq. 12)
  N = Co (Eq. 13, vertical flow) or 0.38*Fr_l^(-0.3)*Co (Eq. 14, horizontal flow)
  psi_N>1 = max(psi_nb, psi_cb);  psi_0.1<N<=1 = max(psi_nbbs, psi_cb);
  psi_N<=0.1 = max(psi_bs, psi_cb)

m [kg/s] mass flow rate, x [-] vapour quality, D [m] hydraulic diameter of the
tube, q [W/m^2] heat transfer rate per unit surface, rhol / rhog [kg/m^3]
densities of the saturated liquid and of the saturated vapour, mul [Pa-s] viscosity
of the liquid, kl [W/m-K] thermal conductivity of the liquid, Cpl [J/kg-K] heat
capacity of the liquid, i_fg [J/kg] latent heat of vaporization, vertical = 1 for
vertical flow, 0 for horizontal flow. Pr_l is capped at 100 and the Reynolds
number uses the liquid mass flow m*(1-x), as in the ThermoCycle implementation.
The branches are blended with the smooth transition function of
tr_factor_Richter2 (half width 25 % of the switch value), as in ThermoCycle.
Validity: saturated flow boiling in a tube, as in the original (no numeric range
  in the Modelica source).
Original: Shah, M. M., "Chart Correlation for Saturated Boiling Heat Transfer:
  Equations and Further Study", ASHRAE Transactions 88(1): 185-196, 1982; as
  implemented in ThermoCycle, equation by equation.
Taken from: ThermoCycle (Modelica, MIT, https://github.com/JeSoftware/
  Thermocycle-library), Components/HeatFlow/HeatTransfer/TwoPhaseCorrelations/
  Shah1982.mo, with Functions/transition_factor.mo and
  SinglePhaseCorrelations/DittusBoelter1930.mo; the same physics is also given in
  ht 1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6), ht/conv_internal.py, turbulent_Dittus_Boelter
  (CSL-0087), but ht has no Shah correlation}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  g_val = 9.80665  "gravitational acceleration [m/s^2]"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)  "two-phase mass flux [kg/m^2/s]"
  Re_l = G*(1-x)*D/mul
  Pr_l = MIN(100, mul*Cpl/kl)  "as in the original: Pr capped at 100"
  hl = 0.023*Re_l^0.8*Pr_l^0.4*kl/D  "liquid-only coefficient (Eq. 5)"
  Co = (1/MAX(x,1E-20) - 1)^0.8*SQRT(rhog/rhol)  "convection number (Eq. 2)"
  Bo = q/(G*i_fg)  "boiling number (Eq. 3)"
  sqrtBo = SQRT(MAX(Bo,1E-20))
  Fr_l = G^2/(rhol^2*g_val*D)  "liquid Froude number (Eq. 4)"
  IF vertical = 1 THEN
    Fr_start = -1E6  "vertical flow: the Froude blend always gives N = Co"
    Fr_stop = 0
  ELSE
    Fr_start = 0.039
    Fr_stop = 0.041
  ENDIF
  N_trans = tr_factor_Richter2(Fr_start, Fr_stop, Fr_l)
  N = (1-N_trans)*(0.38*Fr_l^(-0.3)*Co) + N_trans*Co  "(Eq. 13) and (Eq. 14)"
  F_trans = tr_factor_Richter2(0.875*11E-4, 1.125*11E-4, Bo)
  F = (1-F_trans)*15.43 + F_trans*14.7  "(Eq. 11) and (Eq. 12)"
  psi_cb = 1.8/N^0.8  "convective enhancement (Eq. 8)"
  psi_nb_trans = tr_factor_Richter2(0.875*0.3E-4, 1.125*0.3E-4, Bo)
  psi_nb = (1-psi_nb_trans)*(1 + 46*sqrtBo) + psi_nb_trans*(230*sqrtBo)  "(Eq. 6) and (Eq. 7)"
  psi_N10 = MAX(psi_nb, psi_cb)  "fully nucleate boiling, N > 1"
  psi_nbbs = F*sqrtBo*EXP(2.74*N^(-0.1))  "partly suppressed nucleation (Eq. 9)"
  psi_N05 = MAX(psi_nbbs, psi_cb)  "0.1 < N <= 1"
  psi_bs = F*sqrtBo*EXP(2.47*N^(-0.15))  "suppressed nucleation (Eq. 10)"
  psi_N01 = MAX(psi_bs, psi_cb)  "N <= 0.1"
  psi_0105_trans = tr_factor_Richter2(0.875*0.1, 1.125*0.1, N)
  psi_0105 = (1-psi_0105_trans)*psi_N01 + psi_0105_trans*psi_N05
  psi_0510_trans = tr_factor_Richter2(0.875, 1.125, N)
  psi_0510 = (1-psi_0510_trans)*psi_N05 + psi_0510_trans*psi_N10
  psi_trans = tr_factor_Richter2(0.4375, 0.5625, N)
  psi = (1-psi_trans)*psi_0105 + psi_trans*psi_0510
  h_Shah_1982 = psi*hl
END

FUNCTION h_Gungor_Winterton_1987(m, x, D, q, rhol, rhog, mul, kl, Cpl, i_fg)
{H_GUNGOR_WINTERTON_1987 - heat-transfer coefficient of a saturated fluid boiling
with turbulent forced convection in a tube, simplified general correlation of
Gungor and Winterton.

  h_tp = h_l*[1 + 3000*Bo^0.86 + 1.12*(x/(1-x))^0.75*(rhol/rhog)^0.41]*Term2
  Bo = |q|/(|G|*i_fg)
  Fr_l = G^2/(rhol^2*g*D)
  Term2 = Fr_l^(0.1 - 2*Fr_l) if Fr_l < 0.05, 1 otherwise
  h_l = 0.023*Re_l^0.8*Pr_l^0.4*kl/D (Dittus-Boelter of the liquid flow)

m [kg/s] mass flow rate, x [-] vapour quality, D [m] hydraulic diameter of the
tube, q [W/m^2] heat transfer rate per unit surface, rhol / rhog [kg/m^3]
densities of the saturated liquid and of the saturated vapour, mul [Pa-s] viscosity
of the liquid, kl [W/m-K] thermal conductivity of the liquid, Cpl [J/kg-K] heat
capacity of the liquid, i_fg [J/kg] latent heat of vaporization. The Reynolds
number uses the liquid mass flow m*(1-x) and Pr_l is capped at 100, as in the
ThermoCycle implementation.
Validity: turbulent two-phase flow, as in the original (no numeric range in the
  Modelica source).
Original: Gungor, K. E. and R. H. S. Winterton, "Simplified General Correlation
  for Saturated Flow Boiling and Comparison of Correlations with Data", Chemical
  Engineering Research and Design 65: 148-156, 1987; as implemented in
  ThermoCycle.
Taken from: ThermoCycle (Modelica, MIT, https://github.com/JeSoftware/
  Thermocycle-library), Components/HeatFlow/HeatTransfer/TwoPhaseCorrelations/
  GungorWinterton1987.mo, with SinglePhaseCorrelations/DittusBoelter1930.mo; ht
  1.2.0 (commit 85e0ee6) has no Gungor-Winterton correlation}
  pi_val = 3.141592653589793  "pi, as a local constant (CS-BUG-PI-FUNCTION)"
  g_val = 9.80665  "gravitational acceleration [m/s^2]"
  G = m/(pi_val/4*D^2)  "two-phase mass flux [kg/m^2/s]"
  Re_l = G*(1-x)*D/mul
  Pr_l = MIN(100, mul*Cpl/kl)  "as in the original: Pr capped at 100"
  hl = 0.023*Re_l^0.8*Pr_l^0.4*kl/D  "liquid-only coefficient"
  Fr_l = G^2/(rhol^2*g_val*D)  "liquid Froude number"
  Bo = ABS(q)/(ABS(G)*i_fg)  "boiling number"
  term1 = 1 + 3000*Bo^0.86 + 1.12*(x/(1-x))^0.75*(rhol/rhog)^0.41
  IF Fr_l < 0.05 THEN
    term2 = Fr_l^(0.1 - 2*Fr_l)  "small liquid Froude number: low-velocity correction"
  ELSE
    term2 = 1
  ENDIF
  h_Gungor_Winterton_1987 = hl*term1*term2
END

"!Demonstration program: every correlation is called"
"It is ignored by the future $INCLUDE library:flow_boiling_in_tubes (CS-FEAT-IMPORT)."
"Case A: the input set of the ht doctest of each correlation (published values);"
"for the two ThermoCycle correlations, the same fluid as the ht doctests (CO2 at"
"1 bar, as in the review ht quotes). Case B: one uniform, realistic input set -"
"R134a at 5 C (CoolProp 8.0.0 saturation properties), 0.2 kg/s, quality 0.4,"
"10 mm tube, 4E4 W/m^2, wall 30 K above the saturation temperature. Case C: a low"
"liquid Froude number (0.85 kg/s in a 100 mm tube), which is where the vertical"
"and the horizontal form of Shah 1982 differ. All values are tabulated against"
"ht (or against the Python transcription of the ThermoCycle equations) in the"
"README (verification tables)."

"Case A - input sets of the ht doctests (CO2-like properties, as in the reviews ht quotes)"
LB_m_A = 10
LB_D_A = 0.3
LB_mul_A = 1E-3
LB_kl_A = 0.6
LB_Hvap_A = 2E6
LB_Te_A = 100
LB_q_A = 1E7
LW_m_A = 1
LW_x_A = 0.2
LW_D_A = 0.3
LW_rhol_A = 567
LW_rhog_A = 18.09
LW_kl_A = 0.086
LW_mul_A = 156E-6
LW_sigma_A = 0.02
LW_Hvap_A = 9E5
LW_q_A = 1E5
SM_m_A = 1
SM_D_A = 0.3
SM_rhol_A = 567
SM_rhog_A = 18.09
SM_kl_A = 0.086
SM_mul_A = 156E-6
SM_sigma_A = 0.02
SM_Hvap_A = 9E5
SM_Te_A = 10
SM_q_A = 1E5
TH_rhol_A = 567
TH_rhog_A = 18.09
TH_kl_A = 0.086
TH_kg_A = 0.2
TH_mul_A = 156E-6
TH_mug_A = 1E-5
TH_Cpl_A = 2300
TH_Cpg_A = 1400
TH_sigma_A = 0.02
TH_Hvap_A = 9E5
TH_Psat_A = 1E5
TH_Pc_A = 22E6
TH_D_A = 0.3
TH_q_A = 1E5
TH_x1_A = 0.4
TH_m1_A = 1
TH_x2_A = 0.5
TH_m2_A = 10
YK_m_A = 1
YK_x_A = 0.4
YK_D_A = 0.3
YK_rhol_A = 567
YK_mul_A = 156E-6
YK_sigma_A = 0.02
YK_Hvap_A = 9E5
YK_q_A = 1E4
CB_m_A = 0.106
CB_x_A = 0.2
CB_D_A = 0.0212
CB_rhol_A = 567
CB_rhog_A = 18.09
CB_mul_A = 156E-6
CB_mug_A = 7.11E-6
CB_kl_A = 0.086
CB_Cpl_A = 2730
CB_Hvap_A = 2E5
CB_sigma_A = 0.02
CB_dPsat_A = 1E5
CB_Te_A = 3
LR_m_A = 1
LR_x_A = 0.4
LR_D_A = 0.3
LR_rhol_A = 567
LR_rhog_A = 18.09
LR_mul_A = 156E-6
LR_kl_A = 0.086
LR_Cpl_A = 2300
LR_P_A = 1E6
LR_Pc_A = 22E6
LR_MW_A = 44.02
LR_Te_A = 7
SG_m_A = 1
SG_x_A = 0.4
SG_D_A = 0.01
SG_q_A = 5E4
SG_rhol_A = 567
SG_rhog_A = 18.09
SG_mul_A = 156E-6
SG_kl_A = 0.086
SG_Cpl_A = 2300
SG_ifg_A = 9E5

"h_Lazarek_Black - excess temperature (ht doctest) and heat flux (ht test value)"
h_Lazarek_Black_Te_A = h_Lazarek_Black(LB_m_A, LB_D_A, LB_mul_A, LB_kl_A, LB_Hvap_A, LB_q_A, LB_Te_A, 1)
h_Lazarek_Black_q_A = h_Lazarek_Black(LB_m_A, LB_D_A, LB_mul_A, LB_kl_A, LB_Hvap_A, LB_q_A, LB_Te_A, 0)
"h_Li_Wu - heat flux (ht doctest) and the excess temperature it implies"
h_Li_Wu_q_A = h_Li_Wu(LW_m_A, LW_x_A, LW_D_A, LW_rhol_A, LW_rhog_A, LW_mul_A, LW_kl_A, LW_Hvap_A, LW_sigma_A, LW_q_A, 0, 0)
h_Li_Wu_Te_A = h_Li_Wu(LW_m_A, LW_x_A, LW_D_A, LW_rhol_A, LW_rhog_A, LW_mul_A, LW_kl_A, LW_Hvap_A, LW_sigma_A, LW_q_A, LW_q_A/h_Li_Wu_q_A, 1)
"h_Sun_Mishima - excess temperature (ht doctest) and heat flux (ht test value)"
h_Sun_Mishima_Te_A = h_Sun_Mishima(SM_m_A, SM_D_A, SM_rhol_A, SM_rhog_A, SM_mul_A, SM_kl_A, SM_Hvap_A, SM_sigma_A, SM_q_A, SM_Te_A, 1)
h_Sun_Mishima_q_A = h_Sun_Mishima(SM_m_A, SM_D_A, SM_rhol_A, SM_rhog_A, SM_mul_A, SM_kl_A, SM_Hvap_A, SM_sigma_A, SM_q_A, SM_Te_A, 0)
"h_Thome - two heat fluxes, as in the two cases of the ht test"
h_Thome_q_A = h_Thome(TH_m1_A, TH_x1_A, TH_D_A, TH_rhol_A, TH_rhog_A, TH_mul_A, TH_mug_A, TH_kl_A, TH_kg_A, TH_Cpl_A, TH_Cpg_A, TH_Hvap_A, TH_sigma_A, TH_Psat_A, TH_Pc_A, TH_q_A)
h_Thome_q2_A = h_Thome(TH_m2_A, TH_x2_A, TH_D_A, TH_rhol_A, TH_rhog_A, TH_mul_A, TH_mug_A, TH_kl_A, TH_kg_A, TH_Cpl_A, TH_Cpg_A, TH_Hvap_A, TH_sigma_A, TH_Psat_A, TH_Pc_A, TH_q_A)
"h_Yun_Heo_Kim - heat flux (ht doctest) and the excess temperature it implies"
h_Yun_Heo_Kim_q_A = h_Yun_Heo_Kim(YK_m_A, YK_x_A, YK_D_A, YK_rhol_A, YK_mul_A, YK_Hvap_A, YK_sigma_A, YK_q_A, 0, 0)
h_Yun_Heo_Kim_Te_A = h_Yun_Heo_Kim(YK_m_A, YK_x_A, YK_D_A, YK_rhol_A, YK_mul_A, YK_Hvap_A, YK_sigma_A, YK_q_A, YK_q_A/h_Yun_Heo_Kim_q_A, 1)
"h_Chen_Edelstein"
h_Chen_Edelstein_A = h_Chen_Edelstein(CB_m_A, CB_x_A, CB_D_A, CB_rhol_A, CB_rhog_A, CB_mul_A, CB_mug_A, CB_kl_A, CB_Cpl_A, CB_Hvap_A, CB_sigma_A, CB_dPsat_A, CB_Te_A)
"h_Chen_Bennett"
h_Chen_Bennett_A = h_Chen_Bennett(CB_m_A, CB_x_A, CB_D_A, CB_rhol_A, CB_rhog_A, CB_mul_A, CB_mug_A, CB_kl_A, CB_Cpl_A, CB_Hvap_A, CB_sigma_A, CB_dPsat_A, CB_Te_A)
"h_Liu_Winterton"
h_Liu_Winterton_A = h_Liu_Winterton(LR_m_A, LR_x_A, LR_D_A, LR_rhol_A, LR_rhog_A, LR_mul_A, LR_kl_A, LR_Cpl_A, LR_MW_A, LR_P_A, LR_Pc_A, LR_Te_A)
"h_Shah_1982 - vertical and horizontal flow (identical here: the liquid Froude number is high)"
h_Shah_1982_v_A = h_Shah_1982(SG_m_A, SG_x_A, SG_D_A, SG_q_A, SG_rhol_A, SG_rhog_A, SG_mul_A, SG_kl_A, SG_Cpl_A, SG_ifg_A, 1)
h_Shah_1982_h_A = h_Shah_1982(SG_m_A, SG_x_A, SG_D_A, SG_q_A, SG_rhol_A, SG_rhog_A, SG_mul_A, SG_kl_A, SG_Cpl_A, SG_ifg_A, 0)
"h_Gungor_Winterton_1987"
h_Gungor_Winterton_A = h_Gungor_Winterton_1987(SG_m_A, SG_x_A, SG_D_A, SG_q_A, SG_rhol_A, SG_rhog_A, SG_mul_A, SG_kl_A, SG_Cpl_A, SG_ifg_A)

"Case B - uniform input set: R134a saturated at 5 C (CoolProp 8.0.0)"
m_B = 0.2
x_B = 0.4
D_B = 0.01
q_B = 4E4
Te_B = 30
rhol_B = 1278.0699907738838
rhog_B = 17.1308574901
mul_B = 0.000250111362056
mug_B = 0.0000109110428049
kl_B = 0.0898078137783
kg_B = 0.0119540069634
Cpl_B = 1355.1559599
Cpg_B = 920.594602049
Hvap_B = 194740.148673
sigma_B = 0.0107300565896
dPsat_B = 537322.375747
Psat_B = 3.5E5
Pc_B = 4059276.3737910665
P_B = 3.5E5
MW_B = 102.032

"h_Lazarek_Black - heat flux and excess temperature"
h_Lazarek_Black_q_B = h_Lazarek_Black(m_B, D_B, mul_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, q_B, Te_B, 0)
h_Lazarek_Black_Te_B = h_Lazarek_Black(m_B, D_B, mul_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, q_B, Te_B, 1)
"h_Li_Wu - heat flux and excess temperature"
h_Li_Wu_q_B = h_Li_Wu(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, q_B, Te_B, 0)
h_Li_Wu_Te_B = h_Li_Wu(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, q_B, Te_B, 1)
"h_Sun_Mishima - heat flux and excess temperature"
h_Sun_Mishima_q_B = h_Sun_Mishima(m_B, D_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, q_B, Te_B, 0)
h_Sun_Mishima_Te_B = h_Sun_Mishima(m_B, D_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, q_B, Te_B, 1)
"h_Thome - heat flux given, and excess temperature given (q becomes an unknown)"
h_Thome_q_B = h_Thome(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, mug_B, kl_B, kg_B, Cpl_B, Cpg_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, Psat_B, Pc_B, q_B)
h_Thome_Te_B = h_Thome(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, mug_B, kl_B, kg_B, Cpl_B, Cpg_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, Psat_B, Pc_B, q_Thome_Te_B)
q_Thome_Te_B = h_Thome_Te_B*Te_B  "the equation ht solves by iteration (q = h*Te); q_Thome_Te_B is the unknown (see the .initials)"
"h_Yun_Heo_Kim - heat flux and excess temperature"
h_Yun_Heo_Kim_q_B = h_Yun_Heo_Kim(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, mul_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, q_B, Te_B, 0)
h_Yun_Heo_Kim_Te_B = h_Yun_Heo_Kim(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, mul_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, q_B, Te_B, 1)
"h_Chen_Edelstein"
h_Chen_Edelstein_B = h_Chen_Edelstein(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, mug_B, kl_B, Cpl_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, dPsat_B, Te_B)
"h_Chen_Bennett"
h_Chen_Bennett_B = h_Chen_Bennett(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, mug_B, kl_B, Cpl_B, Hvap_B, sigma_B, dPsat_B, Te_B)
"h_Liu_Winterton"
h_Liu_Winterton_B = h_Liu_Winterton(m_B, x_B, D_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Cpl_B, MW_B, P_B, Pc_B, Te_B)
"h_Shah_1982 - vertical and horizontal flow"
h_Shah_1982_v_B = h_Shah_1982(m_B, x_B, D_B, q_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Cpl_B, Hvap_B, 1)
h_Shah_1982_h_B = h_Shah_1982(m_B, x_B, D_B, q_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Cpl_B, Hvap_B, 0)
"h_Gungor_Winterton_1987"
h_Gungor_Winterton_B = h_Gungor_Winterton_1987(m_B, x_B, D_B, q_B, rhol_B, rhog_B, mul_B, kl_B, Cpl_B, Hvap_B)
"the liquid-only coefficient the two ThermoCycle correlations start from"
hl_B = 0.023*(m_B*(1-x_B)/(3.141592653589793/4*D_B^2)*D_B/mul_B)^0.8*MIN(100, mul_B*Cpl_B/kl_B)^0.4*kl_B/D_B

"Case C - low liquid Froude number (Fr_l = 0.037), CO2 properties as in case A"
m_C = 0.85
x_C = 0.4
D_C = 0.1
q_C = 5E4
rhol_C = 567
rhog_C = 18.09
mul_C = 156E-6
kl_C = 0.086
Cpl_C = 2300
ifg_C = 9E5
Fr_l_C = (m_C/(3.141592653589793/4*D_C^2))^2/(rhol_C^2*9.80665*D_C)  "liquid Froude number [-]"

"h_Shah_1982 - vertical flow (N = Co) and horizontal flow (N = 0.38*Fr_l^-0.3*Co)"
h_Shah_1982_v_C = h_Shah_1982(m_C, x_C, D_C, q_C, rhol_C, rhog_C, mul_C, kl_C, Cpl_C, ifg_C, 1)
h_Shah_1982_h_C = h_Shah_1982(m_C, x_C, D_C, q_C, rhol_C, rhog_C, mul_C, kl_C, Cpl_C, ifg_C, 0)
"h_Gungor_Winterton_1987 - the low-Froude correction term is active here"
h_Gungor_Winterton_C = h_Gungor_Winterton_1987(m_C, x_C, D_C, q_C, rhol_C, rhog_C, mul_C, kl_C, Cpl_C, ifg_C)
